  "query_preview": "..\n(Sunday night through Friday)\nIssued at 329 AM EST Sat Nov 14 2026\n\nAll in all, the long term period (Sunday Night through the end of \nthe work week) should be largely uneventful for North and Central \nGeorgia. A trough situated over New England down the Mid-Atlantic \nwill continue eastward and another shortwave trough will swing \nthrough the ArkLaMiss. At the surface, the associated low pressure \nsystem will translate across the northern Gulf Coast while pushing \neastward. At this time, most of the moisture associated with this \nsystem will be shunted south of our area as strong high pressure \nbuilds and extends south into the TN Valley/Southeast states. Behind \nthe low pressure and departing shortwave (around mid-week), \namplified mid-level ridging will build and remain through the end of \nthe work week. Thus, the effects of high pressure and ridging aloft \nwill keep conditions mostly dry and comfortable, slowly warming \ntemperatures each day. Low temperatures will start off at or just \nbelow freezing (Sunday Night/Monday Morning) climbing back into the \n40s to near 50 by late week. Daytime temperatures will behave \nsimilarly, starting off in the 50s to near 60s on Monday, warming \ninto the 70s by Friday.",
